# trusted-requesters.txt.example — format template for the AD-1(c) roster
# (#1540 S1). Copy entries from here into the LIVE `trusted-requesters.txt`
# (a protected surface); this file itself is documentation only and is
# never read by the gate.
#
# Membership in the live roster confers REQUESTER trust only — it never
# confers approver authority. A trusted requester's issue needs no live
# human-authorization act to be selected as work; it says nothing about who
# may APPROVE work.
#
# Bot logins are rejected by the parser: an entry ending in "[bot]", or one
# matching BOT_ACCOUNTS, is skipped with a diagnostic. The roster is for
# people.
#
# ── Form 1: an explicit login ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every entry requires per-entry provenance — who added it, when, and why —
# so a reviewer can audit each grant on its own terms, not just the file as
# a whole:
#
#   SomeTrustedContributor   # added-by: ScottThurlow added: 2026-09-01 why: frequent triager, vetted manually
#
# ── Form 2: a permission-tier delegation ─────────────────────────────────
# The REQUIRED "tier:" prefix is the discriminator between a login and a
# tier — a bare `write` on its own line is parsed as A LOGIN NAMED "write",
# not as a tier. This is deliberate: GitHub logins and permission names
# occupy the same string space (`admin` is itself a valid GitHub login), so
# a format that distinguished them by VALUE would make the roster's meaning
# depend on GitHub's account namespace. The prefix makes the distinction
# lexical and local.
#
#   tier:write              # added-by: ScottThurlow added: 2026-09-01 why: all push-access collaborators are pre-vetted
#
# <name> is one of GitHub's own five permission levels, used directly —
# read, triage, write, maintain, admin — with NO HOS mapping table. This is
# a STANDING DELEGATION, not a one-time decision: membership is resolved
# LIVE against GitHub's collaborator-permission API at every gate cycle,
# so it tracks who currently holds that permission WITHOUT a new commit
# here — a collaborator promoted or demoted takes effect on the next cycle.
#
# Matching is EXACT, not hierarchical: `tier:write` admits collaborators
# whose permission is exactly `write` — it does NOT also admit `maintain`
# or `admin`. If you want "write and above" trusted, list all three tiers
# you intend, e.g.:
#
#   tier:write               # added-by: ScottThurlow added: 2026-09-01 why: see above
#   tier:maintain             # added-by: ScottThurlow added: 2026-09-01 why: include maintainers too
#   tier:admin                # added-by: ScottThurlow added: 2026-09-01 why: include admins too
#
# An unrecognised tier name (e.g. `tier:owner`, `tier:collaborator`) is
# skipped with a diagnostic naming the five legal values — it is never
# silently ignored and never passed through to the API to see what
# happens.
#
# HOS ships ZERO trusted tiers, exactly as it ships zero trusted logins.
# Which tiers (if any) a project trusts is the project owner's decision,
# not an HOS default.
